        content: 'Vertical Scaling (Scale Up):\n• Add more power to existing machine (CPU, RAM, storage)\n• Simpler, no code changes needed\n• Has hardware limits\n• Single point of failure\n\nHorizontal Scaling (Scale Out):\n• Add more machines to the pool\n• Theoretically unlimited scaling\n• Requires load balancing\n• More complex but more resilient\n\nModern systems use horizontal scaling with stateless services.',
        codeExample: '// Stateless service design for horizontal scaling\nclass UserService {\n  // No instance state - all state in external stores\n  async getUser(userId: string) {\n    // Read from shared database/cache\n    return await redis.get(`user:${userId}`) \n      || await database.users.findById(userId);\n  }\n}\n\n// Any instance can handle any request\n// Load balancer distributes traffic evenly',
