        content: 'A load balancer distributes incoming traffic across multiple backend servers so no single server is overwhelmed.\n\nLayer 4 (Transport):\n• Routes by IP/port — fast, protocol-agnostic\n• Cannot inspect payload\n• Examples: AWS NLB, HAProxy in TCP mode\n\nLayer 7 (Application):\n• Routes by HTTP path, headers, cookies\n• Can do TLS termination, compression, caching\n• Examples: NGINX, Envoy, AWS ALB\n\nAlgorithms:\n• Round Robin — simplest, ignores load\n• Least Connections — picks least-busy server\n• Weighted — heavier servers get more traffic\n• IP Hash — same client → same server (sticky)\n• Power of Two Choices — random pick of 2, send to less loaded; near-optimal with low overhead\n\nSticky sessions break stateless scaling — prefer external session stores instead.',
        codeExample: '// Round-robin selection\nlet idx = 0;\nfunction nextServer(servers) {\n  const s = servers[idx % servers.length];\n  idx++;\n  return s;\n}\n\n// Power of two choices (better load distribution)\nfunction powerOfTwo(servers) {\n  const a = servers[Math.floor(Math.random() * servers.length)];\n  const b = servers[Math.floor(Math.random() * servers.length)];\n  return a.activeConns < b.activeConns ? a : b;\n}',
