        content: 'Components of a Google-like search at modest scale:\n\n1. Crawler:\n• URL frontier (priority queue with politeness windows per domain)\n• Fetcher pool (HTTP, robots.txt-aware)\n• Parser extracts text + outlinks\n• Dedup via SimHash or shingling (avoid storing duplicate pages)\n\n2. Indexer:\n• Tokenize, normalize (lowercase, stem, remove stopwords)\n• Build inverted index: term → posting list (doc IDs + positions + frequency)\n• Distributed across shards (by term hash or doc ID range)\n• Compress posting lists (delta encoding, variable-byte)\n\n3. Query engine:\n• Parse query, expand synonyms\n• Fan out to shards, merge top-K results\n• Score each candidate (TF-IDF, BM25)\n• Rerank top results with ranking signals (PageRank, freshness, click-through, user features)\n\n4. Ranking signals:\n• PageRank — link structure as authority\n• BM25 — term frequency × inverse document frequency, with length normalization\n• Click-through rate from logs\n• Freshness, locality, user history\n\n5. Serving:\n• Cache popular query results\n• Suggest / autocomplete via trie or Marisa\n\nRule of thumb: indexes are huge; design every stage assuming you can\'t fit in memory and must shard.',
